 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Over-smoothing:** Naively adopting amplitude and phase discrepancies without distinguishing between low and high frequencies can bias the splatting towards undesirable artifacts and restrict Gaussian densification [S2].
-- **Precision:** The use of `arctan` for phase calculation requires handling cases where $\text{Re}(u, v) \approx 0$ to avoid division by zero or incorrect quadrant determination [G].
-- **Boundaries:** The DFT assumes periodic boundary conditions, which may introduce artifacts at image borders if not handled [G].
+*   **Over-reconstruction Bias:** Naively adopting amplitude and phase discrepancies without distinguishing between low and high frequencies can bias the splatting towards undesirable artifacts and restrict Gaussian densification [S2]. This is addressed in variants like FreGS via frequency annealing [S2].
+*   **Gradient Vanishing:** In well-reconstructed regions, standard L1 loss yields small gradients, preventing densification. FR mitigates this by maintaining higher gradients in over-reconstructed regions [S2].
 
